prompt":"In a commuting survey, people who bike to work had a mean commute time of 38 minutes (SD about 12) and people who take the bus had a mean of 35 minutes (SD about 12). A student claims: \"people who bike to work always have a higher commute time than people who take the bus.\" Which statement best justifies whether the data support this claim?","mcq_form":{"options":[{"text":"The claim is correct because people who bike to work clearly did better on commute time.","correct":false,"misconception":"restates_claim_without_evidence","misconception_source":{"tag":"restates_claim_without_evidence","label":"Restated the claim without citing statistical evidence","evidence":"documented_cr","ek_codes":[],"sources":[{"kind":"fact_pack","ref":"AP_STATISTICS_2027_CED_FACT_PACK.md S4/S6 (skill 4.B; task verbs) + S10 'General exam-wide conventions'","note":"a justification must reference the statistical evidence, not merely restate the claim"}]}},{"text":"Because people who bike to work had a higher mean commute time, being in people who bike to work causes a higher commute time.","correct":false,"misconception":"association_implies_causation","misconception_source":{"tag":"association_implies_causation","label":"Interpreted an observational association as causation","evidence":"documented_cr","ek_codes":["5.2.A.4"],"sources":[{"kind":"fact_pack","ref":"AP_STATISTICS_2027_CED_FACT_PACK.md S10 Unit 5 (5.2)","note":"EK 5.2.A.4: correlation does not imply causation (stated explicitly)"},{"kind":"fact_pack","ref":"AP_STATISTICS_2027_CED_FACT_PACK.md S10 Unit 1 (1.10-1.13)","note":"observational studies (no random assignment) do not support causal claims"}]}},{"text":"Since the mean for people who bike to work (38) is greater than for people who take the bus (35), every member of people who bike to work must have a higher commute time than every member of people who take the bus.","correct":false,"misconception":"ignores_variability_claims_every_value","misconception_source":{