dom assignment) do not support causal claims"}]}},{"text":"On average students who study mostly at night had a higher self-reported focus rating (mean 51 vs 45), so the data support a typical difference; but because both SDs are about 10, the distributions overlap substantially, so the data do not support a claim that students who study mostly at night are always higher.","correct":true,"misconception":null},{"text":"These results prove that students who study mostly at night will always outperform students who study mostly in the morning on self-reported focus rating in any future study or population.","correct":false,"misconception":"over_generalizes_beyond_data","misconception_source":{"tag":"over_generalizes_beyond_data","label":"Over-generalized beyond the data using definitive language","evidence":"documented_cr","ek_codes":[],"sources":[{"kind":"fact_pack","ref":"AP_STATISTICS_2027_CED_FACT_PACK.md S10 'General exam-wide conventions'","note":"2025 CR Report: conclusions must use non-definitive language ('convincing evidence'), never 'proves'/'always'; and must not generalize beyond the studied population"}]}},{"text":"The claim is correct because students who study mostly at night clearly did better on self-reported focus rating.","correct":false,"misconception":"restates_claim_without_evidence","misconception_source":{"tag":"restates_claim_without_evidence","label":"Restated the claim without citing statistical evidence","evidence":"documented_cr","ek_codes":[],"sources":[{"kind":"fact_pack","ref":"AP_STATISTICS_2027_CED_FACT_PACK.md S4/S6 (skill 4.B; task verbs) + S10 'General exam-wide conventions'","note":"a justification must reference the statistical evidence, not merely restate the claim"}]}}]},"parts":[{"part_key":"part-a","prompt":"In a survey of study habits, students who study mostly at night had a mean self-reported focus rating of 51 points (SD about 10) and students who study mostly in the morning had a mean of 45 points (SD about 10). A student